From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] net: stmmac: dwmac-sun55i: program the A733 syscon after the MAC reset

On the A733 (sun60iw2) the "syscon" register this glue programs is GMAC_CFG,
the first register of the GMAC's own glue block at 0x04508000 -- not a slot in
the shared system-control block as on the A523. That difference is not just an
offset, which is all the driver models today: it puts the register inside the
MAC's reset domain.

sun55i_gmac200_probe() writes the register and only then calls
devm_stmmac_pltfr_probe(), and stmmac_dvr_probe() begins with

	reset_control_assert(priv->plat->stmmac_rst);
	reset_control_deassert(priv->plat->stmmac_rst);

so on the A733 every value this driver writes is wiped before the MAC is used.
The register returns to its reset default 0x00008000, whose bit 15 PHY_SELECT
selects the *internal* PHY (A733 UM). The MDIO scan
later in that same probe then talks to a PHY that does not exist on this SoC,
and the external bus stays silent:

	dwmac-sun55i 4500000.ethernet: User ID: 0x20, Synopsys ID: 0x53
	mdio_bus stmmac-7: MDIO device at address 1 is missing.
	eth0: cannot attach to PHY (error: -ENODEV)

Measured on a Radxa Cubie A7S (8 GiB), mainline 6.18.36, 2026-07-21. The MAC
itself is healthy (MAC_VERSION 0x2053), the PHY rail is on (AXP318W reg 0x11 =
0x1f, SWOUT1 set), its 25 MHz reference runs (GMAC0_PHY_CLK_REG 0x80000005),
the block is out of reset (GMAC0_BGR_REG 0x00030001), the RGMII pins are muxed
(PH_CFG0/CFG1 0x55555555) and the MDIO line idles high -- yet a hand-driven
clause-22 read over MAC_MDIO_Address/Data returns 0xFFFF on all 32 addresses.
Writing GMAC_CFG = 0x00011146 by hand and repeating that scan returns 0x7B74 at
address 1 immediately, and the driver reaches a 1 Gbps/Full link with a DHCP
lease and bidirectional ping. Unbinding and re-binding the driver reproduces
the failure exactly: 0x00000000 while unbound, 0x00008000 after bind.

Fix it where the asymmetry is, in the per-variant match data: pulse the reset
in the glue, program the register afterwards, and take the reset away from
stmmac so it cannot undo the write. The A523 keeps its current behaviour --
its register is outside the reset domain and needs none of this.

The Allwinner BSP glue (dwmac-sunxi.c) orders it the same way: its
sunxi_dwmac_clk_init() deasserts the resets and only then does
sunxi_dwmac_hw_init() call set_syscon().

Note this driver writes no MAC-side delays of its own, so with nothing in the DT
the programmed value is 0x00000006 (EPIT | ETCS_INT_GMII) rather than the
vendor's 0x00011146 (which carries TX 12 / RX 10).

[Corrected 2026-07-21, after this patch was merged: the sentence that stood here
claimed the delay is added by the Maxio PHY instead. It is not. Measured on the
board, a link with no MAC-side delay trains at 1 Gbps and then loses 100 % of
its frames; `arm64-dts-a733-gmac0-measured-rgmii-delays` adds tx-internal-delay-ps = 700 / rx-internal-delay-ps = 1000
from a sweep. The PHY does contribute its RX 2 ns, but from the RXDLY strap, not
from driver programming -- `net-phy-maxio-drop-phy-side-rgmii-delay` removed that programming after the datasheet
showed the register it wrote does not exist.] Suspend/resume is not addressed here: with the reset no longer held by
stmmac, a resume path that powers the block down would need the register
reprogrammed.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-sun55i.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-sun55i.c
@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@
 #include <linux/property.h>
 #include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include <linux/regulator/consumer.h>
+#include <linux/reset.h>
 #include <linux/stmmac.h>
 
 #include "stmmac.h"
@@ -36,6 +37,11 @@
  */
 struct sun55i_gmac_variant {
 	u32 syscon_offset;
+	/*
+	 * True when that register sits inside the GMAC block itself and is
+	 * therefore cleared by the MAC reset. See sun55i_gmac200_probe().
+	 */
+	bool syscon_in_reset_domain;
 };
 
 static const struct sun55i_gmac_variant sun55i_a523_gmac200 = {
@@ -44,6 +50,7 @@
 
 static const struct sun55i_gmac_variant sun60iw2_gmac200 = {
 	.syscon_offset = 0x00,
+	.syscon_in_reset_domain = true,
 };
 
 /* RMII specific bits */
@@ -126,12 +133,17 @@
 
 static int sun55i_gmac200_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
+	const struct sun55i_gmac_variant *variant;
 	struct plat_stmmacenet_data *plat_dat;
 	struct stmmac_resources stmmac_res;
 	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
 	struct clk *clk;
 	int ret;
 
+	variant = device_get_match_data(dev);
+	if (!variant)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
 	ret = stmmac_get_platform_resources(pdev, &stmmac_res);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
@@ -144,6 +156,26 @@
 	plat_dat->flags |= STMMAC_FLAG_SPH_DISABLE;
 	plat_dat->host_dma_width = 32;
 
+	/*
+	 * On the A733 the syscon register is GMAC_CFG, the first register of
+	 * the GMAC's own glue block, so it shares the MAC's reset domain: the
+	 * assert/deassert that stmmac_dvr_probe() performs would clear it back
+	 * to its reset default, and that default selects the *internal* PHY
+	 * (PHY_SELECT). The MDIO scan that follows inside the same probe would
+	 * then find nothing on the external bus. Pulse the reset here, program
+	 * the register afterwards, and take the reset away from stmmac so it
+	 * cannot undo the write. On the A523 the register lives in the shared
+	 * system-control block, outside that reset domain, and needs none of
+	 * this.
+	 */
+	if (variant->syscon_in_reset_domain && plat_dat->stmmac_rst) {
+		ret = reset_control_assert(plat_dat->stmmac_rst);
+		reset_control_deassert(plat_dat->stmmac_rst);
+		if (ret == -ENOTSUPP)
+			reset_control_reset(plat_dat->stmmac_rst);
+		plat_dat->stmmac_rst = NULL;
+	}
+
 	ret = sun55i_gmac200_set_syscon(dev, plat_dat);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
